ographs were available. Fifth Avenue runs 29 degrees east of north (Manhattan's grid) and the whole complex is set to it: the four-block limestone front (80th to 84th Street), McKim, Mead & White's wings either side of Richard Morris Hunt's taller central pavilion (1902) with its three arches between four pairs of columns, the uncarved blocks above them, the grand stair, the plaza's fountains and trees; the Great Hall's saucer domes; the older core behind under gallery skylights, with Vaux and Mould's 1880 red-brick Gothic front where it survives inside the Lehman court; Roche-Dinkeloo's wings toward the park: the Temple of Dendur's sloping glass wall to the north, the American Wing's glass garden court to the northwest, the Lehman Wing's glass pyramid on the axis to the west, the Rockefeller and Wallace wings with their glass wall to the south and the Cantor roof garden; Cleopatra's Needle behind; Central Park round the back. Guessed: every depth and height (front about 23 m, the pavilion 32 m), the skylight rows, the wings' exact outlines."}